","To calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction, we can use the formula:

ΔS = Σ(n * ΔS°) - Σ(n * ΔS°)

where n is the number of moles of each substance and ΔS° is the standard entropy of each substance.

The reaction is:

3CO(g) + O2(g) → 3CO2(g)

We need the standard entropies (ΔS°) of CO, O2, and CO2 at 298 K. From standard thermodynamic tables, we find:

ΔS°(CO) = 227.3 J/mol·K
ΔS°(O2) = 205.1 J/mol·K
ΔS°(CO2) = 238.1 J/mol·K

Now we can calculate the change in entropy:

ΔS = (3 * ΔS°(CO2)) - (3 * ΔS°(CO) + 1 * ΔS°(O2))

ΔS = (3 * 238.1 J/mol·K) - (3 * 227.3 J/mol·K + 1 * 205.1 J/mol·K)

ΔS = 714.3 J/mol·K - (681.9 J/mol·K + 205.1 J/mol·K)

