   \[ \text{Cu} \rightarrow \text{Cu}^{2+} + 2e^- \]  
   Standard standard potential \( E^\circ_{\text{anode}} = -0.342 \text{ V} \) (oxidation potential of Cu)

2. **Cathode-half reaction (reduction):**  
   \[ \text{Ag}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Ag} \]  
   Standard standard potential \( E^\circ_{\text{cathode}} = +0.7996 \text{ V} \) (reduction potential of Ag)

The standard cell potential \( E^\circ_{\text{cell}} \) is the difference between the cathode and anode potentials:
\[
E^\circ_{\text{cell}} = E^\circ_{\text{cathode}} - E^\circ_{\text{anode}} = 0.7996 \text{ V} - (-0..342 \text{ V}) = 1.1416 \text{ V}
\]

Now, we need to find the actual cell potential \( E_{\text{cell}} \) under the given concentrations of \( \text{Cu}^{2+} \) and \( \text{Ag}^{2+} \). We use the Nernst equation:
\[
E_{\text{cell}} = E^\circ_{\text{cell}} - \frac{0.0591}{n} \log \left( \frac{[\text{Ag}^{2+}]}{[\text{Cu}^{2+}]} \right)
