","When \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) gas is passed into a mixture of \( \text{Mn}^{2+}, \text{Ni}^{2+}, \text{Cu}^{2+}, \) and \( \text{Hg}^{2+} \) ions in an acidic aqueous solution, the following precipitates form:  
- **Cu(s):** \( \text{Cu}^{2+} + 2\text{H}_2\text{S} \rightarrow \text{Cu(s)} + 2\text{H}_2\text{O} \) (Prepsitates as solid Cu.)  
- **Hg(s):** \( \text{Hg}^{2+} + 2\text{H}_2\text{S} \rightarrow \text{Hg(s)} + 2\text{H}_2\text{O} \) (Prepsates as solid Hg.)  
- **Mn(s):** \( \text{Mn}^{2+} + 2\text{H}_2\text{S} \rightarrow \text{Mn(s)} + 2\text{H}_2\text{O} \) (Prepsates as solid Mn.)  

- **Ni(s):** \( \text{Ni}^{2+} + 2\text{H}_2\text{S} \rightarrow \text{Ni(s)} + 2\text{H}_2\text{O} \) (Prepsates as solid Ni.)  

Thus, all the sulfides precipitate in acidic solution.  

The correct answer is:  
\[ \text{Cu}^{2-} \), \( \text{Hg}^{2-} \), \( \text{Mn}^{2-} \), and \( \text{Ni}^{2-} \).  

**Note:** The solubility of metal sulfides depends on the concentration of \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) and the pH.  
- The solubility of metal sulfides is generally higher at higher pH levels.  
- The solubility of metal sulfides is generally lower at lower pH levels.  

Thus, in an acidic solution, the solubilities of these sulfides are very low, and they precipitate as solids.  

