1. **Definitions:**
   - For any ordinal \(\alpha\), \(L_\alpha\) is the set space bounded by \(\alpha\), i.e., \(\{x : x \subset \alpha, x \text{ is countable}}\).
   - For any ordinal \(\alpha\), \(V_\alpha\) is the set space bounded by \(\alpha\), i.e., \(\{x : x \subset \alpha, x \text{is bounded by } \alpha\}\).
   - For any ordinal \(\alpha\), \(\beta_\alpha\) is the smallest ordinal such that \(\beta_\alpha = \lim_{\alpha} \pi_\alpha\), where \(\pi_\alpha\) is the projection of \(\alpha\) onto \(\alpha\) on the set space \(L_\alpha\).

2. **Conclusion:**
   The statement is true. The equality \(\|L_\alpha\| = \|V_\alpha\|\) holds if and only if \(\alpha = \beta_\alpha\). This is a fundamental result in set space theory. 

