</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The decay length of an evanescent wave is given by:
\[
\delta = \frac{lambda}{2\pi (n_1 - n_2)}
\]
where:
- \(\lambda\) is the wavelength,
- \(n_1\) is the refractive index of the material where the evanescent wave is propagating,
- \(n_2\) is the refractive index of the material outside the interface.

In this case, the evanescent wave is propagating in the cladding layer, which is Ga\(_{0.2}\)Al\(_{.8}\)As. The refractive index difference is given as \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \sim 0.3\), where \(n_{\text{eff}}\) is the effective refractive index of the cladding and \(n_f\) is the refractive index of the core (GaAs).

Substituting the given values:
\[
\delta = \frac{1 \mu m}{2\pi (0.3)} = \frac{1 \times 10^{-6}}{0.6028 \times 0.3} = \frac{1 \times 10^{-6}}{0.1809} \approx 5.54 \times 10^{-7} \text{ m}
\]

Thus, the decay length is \(\boxed{554 \text{ nm}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

