\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left( \frac{\partial 0}{\partial y} - \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial 0} \right) \mathbf{i} + \left( \frac{\partial V_x}{\partial 0} - \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial 0} \right) \mathbf{j} + \left( \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial 0}{\partial x} \right) \mathbf{k}.
\]
Since the derivatives with respect to \(z\) are zero, the \(x\) and \(y\) components vanish:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = 0 \mathbf{i} + 0 \mathbf{j} + \left( \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x} - 0 \right) \mathbf{k}.
\]
Thus, the curl simplifies to:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left( \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x} \right) \mathbf{k} = \left( \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x} \right) \mathbf{k} = 0 \mathbf{k}.
\]
However, the correct expression for the curl in Cartesian coordinates is:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left( \frac{\partial V_z}{\partial y} - \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial z} \right) \mathbf{i} + \left( \frac{\partial V_1}{\partial z} - \frac{\partial V_1}{\partial z} \right) \mathbf{j} + \left( \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_z}{\partial x} \right) \mathbf{k}.
\]
