The lifetime of a particle is related to its width \(\Gamma\) by the formula:  
\[
\Gamma = \frac{\hbar}{2\tau}
\]  
where \(\hbar\) is the reduced Planck constant (\(\approx 1.054 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J s}\)) and \(\tau\) is the lifetime.  

The width \(\Gamma\) is related to the width of the energy spectrum due(\(\Delta E\)) of the particle. The uncertainty in the energy \(\Delta E\) is related to the lifetime \(\tau\) by:  
\[
\Delta E \approx \frac{\hbar \Gamma}{2} = \frac{\hbar^2}{4\tau}
\]  
Substituting \(\hbar \approx 1.054 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J s}\) and converting \(\tau\) to seconds:  
\[
\Delta E \approx \frac{(1.054 \times 10^{-34})^2}{4 \times (2.0 \times 10^{-23})} = 1.3 \times 10^{-10} \, \text{eV}
\]  
Thus, the uncertainty in the energy is \(\boxed{1.3 \times 10^{-10} \, \text{eV}}\).
